  "shorts": [
    {
      "short_id": "short262",
      "angle": "The hospital did not explicitly refuse screening; it gave a critically ill woman a number and never reached it while she waited.",
      "avoids": "No causal claim that waiting caused death; no depiction of the patient; no claim that EMTALA guarantees good treatment.",
      "funnel_question_left_for_longform": "What does emergency law require a hospital to do, and what finding did the appeals court decline to review?",
      "loop": true,
      "loop_join": "The last line returns to the untouched number from the opening.",
      "era": "Puerto Rico, 1991",
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "L1",
          "text": "A sixty-five-year-old widow entered a Puerto Rico emergency room with chills, cold sweat, dizziness, and chest pains. At the desk, she received a number.",
          "delivery": "intense",
          "source_lines": [
            "On the morning of the sixth of September, 1991, a sixty-five-year-old widow in Puerto Rico woke up feeling, in her son's words, real bad."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L2",
          "text": "The hospital's position was precise: it had not denied her screening or treatment and would have attended to her if she had waited.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "The hospital said it had neither denied Ms. Gonzalez an initial screening nor refused her essential treatment."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L3",
          "text": "But the jury heard evidence that she arrived in critical condition and received what the court called only a high number and a cold shoulder.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "A high number and a cold shoulder."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L4",
          "text": "The court said a complete failure to attend someone showing symptoms widely understood as an acute threat can itself deny an appropriate screening.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Regardless of motive, the court wrote, a complete failure to attend a patient who presents a condition that practically everyone knows may indicate an immediate and acute threat to life can constitute a denial of an appropriate medical screening examination."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L5",
          "text": "That is constructive refusal: nobody says no, yet the system never reaches the person standing inside it asking for care.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Its point was that it gave the patient a number, and would have ministered to her had she waited."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L6",
          "text": "This Short does not claim that the wait caused her death. The legal finding here concerns what happened while she waited.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "It plays no part in what this case decided."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L7",
          "text": "EMTALA promises a screening procedure administered even-handedly, not perfect treatment and not a guarantee that every patient is seen simultaneously.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "It does not guarantee treatment."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L8",
          "text": "The case began with a number handed across a desk, and it ends with the same number still waiting to be called.",
          "delivery": "calm",
          "source_lines": [
            "A high number and a cold shoulder."
          ],
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
