    {
      "short_id": "short263",
      "angle": "The court treated the word appropriate as undefined and built an even-handed screening rule rather than a quality guarantee.",
      "avoids": "No causal claim that waiting caused death; no depiction of the patient; no claim that EMTALA guarantees good treatment.",
      "funnel_question_left_for_longform": "If the rule requires sameness, what happens when a hospital treats every similar patient equally badly?",
      "loop": true,
      "loop_join": "The last line returns to the undefined word in the hook.",
      "era": "Puerto Rico, 1991",
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "L1",
          "text": "Congress required an appropriate medical screening in emergency rooms. The court immediately faced a problem: the statute never explained what appropriate actually meant.",
          "delivery": "intense",
          "source_lines": [
            "EMTALA requires an appropriate medical screening, but does not explain what constitutes one."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L2",
          "text": "The opinion called appropriate one of the most wonderful weasel words in the dictionary, useful because nobody publicly argues for inappropriate treatment.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Appropriate is one of the most wonderful weasel words in the dictionary, and a great aid to the resolution of disputed issues in the drafting of legislation."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L3",
          "text": "But the joke did not decide the case. The court still needed a legal test that hospitals and patients could understand.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Who, after all, can be found to stand up for inappropriate treatment or actions of any sort?"
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L4",
          "text": "Its answer began with a real screening procedure reasonably designed to identify critical conditions in people who arrive with symptoms.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "First: a participating hospital must afford an appropriate medical screening to all persons who come to its emergency room seeking medical assistance."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L5",
          "text": "Then came the controlling comparison: patients presenting substantially similar complaints must receive that level of screening uniformly.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Then the compression."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L6",
          "text": "The essence, the court said, is that some procedure exists and that the hospital administers it even-handedly.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "The essence of this requirement is that there be some screening procedure, and that it be administered even-handedly."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L7",
          "text": "That does not turn federal law into a promise of excellent medicine. A hospital can examine everyone badly without violating this particular rule.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "A hospital that examines you badly has not broken this law."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L8",
          "text": "So appropriate was not defined as best. It was defined through procedure and sameness, returning us to the slippery word that opened the case.",
          "delivery": "calm",
          "source_lines": [
            "The adjectival phrase, the court observed, is not self-defining."
          ],
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
